WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 59: The Bath School Tragedy: The Forgotten Catastrophe of 1927

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.760
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:18.960
[Rebecca] On the sunny spring morning of May 18, 1927, the quiet farming community of Bath Township, Michigan, was preparing for eighth grade graduation at the modern Bath Consolidated School.

4
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.800
[Rebecca] But beneath the floorboards of the North Wing lay a monstrous secret.

5
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:29.760
[Rebecca] Hundreds of pounds of dynamite and pyrotol, secretly wired by the school board's own treasurer, Andrew Cahell.

6
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:39.920
[Rebecca] At 9 45 a.m., an electrical alarm clock triggered a devastating explosion that collapsed the school, killing 38 children and six adults.

7
00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.240
[Rebecca] It remains the deadliest act of mass murder at an American school.

8
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:51.440
[Rebecca] George brings us the harrowing and heroic story of the Bath School disaster.

9
00:00:51.440 --> 00:01:01.680
[George] In the 1920s, Bath Township, Michigan, was a quiet, tight-knit agricultural hamlet located 10 miles northeast of the state capital of Lansing.

10
00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:12.800
[George] The community was composed primarily of hardworking dairy farmers, orchardists, and shopkeepers whose lives revolved around their families, church socials, and local schools.

11
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:27.920
[George] In 1922, progressive citizens voted to modernize their educational system by consolidating several scattered, drafty one-room rural schoolhouses into a single, state-of-the-art brick building, the Bath Consolidated School.

12
00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:40.160
[George] The handsome two story brick edifice opened with great civic pride, housing grades 1 through 12 under one roof, equipped with central steam heating, modern indoor plumbing, and an auditorium.

13
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:45.600
[George] To fund the construction, township voters approved a dedicated property tax levy.

14
00:01:45.600 --> 00:02:01.240
[George] While most residents gladly paid the modest assessment to provide their children with a superior education, a small handful of taxpayers grumbled about the financial burden, none more bitterly than a 54-year old farmer and trained electrician named Andrew Keho.

15
00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:12.680
[George] The construction of the modern consolidated school had been approved by an overwhelming majority of local voters who recognized that the era of scattered, drafty one-room schoolhouses was passing.

16
00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:24.600
[George] The handsome building boasted six spacious classrooms, a domestic science lab, a library, and modern indoor plumbing, standing as a proud beacon of progress in rural Clinton County.

17
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:32.760
[George] Parents were delighted that their children would receive a comprehensive education from dedicated, accredited teachers who shared a passion for learning.

18
00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:39.160
[George] Andrew Kehoe was a prosperous, meticulous, but deeply resentful and short-tempered man.

19
00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:52.520
[George] Educated in electrical engineering at Michigan State College, he possessed extraordinary mechanical skill, capable of repairing complex farm machinery, rewiring motors, and designing intricate timing circuits.

20
00:02:52.520 --> 00:03:06.440
[George] In 1919, he bought a prime 185-acre farm in Bath, but proved to be an inept farmer who frequently quarreled with neighbors, beat his horses in fits of rage, and harbored delusions of persecution.

21
00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:20.840
[George] When his farm fell into deep mortgage foreclosure and his wife Nellie developed severe chronic tuberculosis requiring expensive hospital care, Keho placed the blame for his financial ruin entirely upon the school property taxes.

22
00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:33.800
[George] Keho managed to get elected treasurer of the school board in 1924, where he obsessively opposed any expenditure, no matter how small, arguing furiously over buying pencils or fixing plumbing.

23
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:43.320
[George] When he was defeated for township clerk in the spring election of 1927, his simmering paranoia curdled into murderous vengeance against the entire community.

24
00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:48.800
[George] Keho's brooding resentment isolated him from the warm social fabric of the rural township.

25
00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:58.800
[George] He spent long, solitary evenings pacing his porch, obsessing over every line of the school board ledger and drafting angry letters to local tax officials.

26
00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:13.760
[George] When his beloved orchard trees failed to yield fruit and his prized team of farm horses died from improper care, Keho refused to accept responsibility, choosing instead to blame his misfortune on the community leaders who supported the school.

27
00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:28.560
[George] Beginning in the autumn of 1926, Keho utilized his position as school board maintenance trustee, which gave him unrestricted, solitary access to the school basement to execute a meticulously planned act of domestic terrorism.

28
00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:45.360
[George] Over a period of seven months, Keho purchased more than a ton of pyrotal, an explosive military surplus compound made of sawdust and nitrocellulose, along with hundreds of pounds of commercial dynamite, claiming he needed the explosives to clear tree stumps on his farm.

29
00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:57.440
[George] Under cover of night and during weekend maintenance inspections, Keho painstakingly crept through the narrow, unventilated crawl spaces beneath the first-floor classrooms of the Bath Consolidated School.

30
00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:05.200
[George] He drilled holes through foundational timbers, installed wooden troughs, and laid hundreds of yards of hidden copper wiring.

31
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:11.920
[George] He packed over 500 pounds of dynamite and pyrotol beneath the floor joists of the north wing and south wing.

32
00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:21.840
[George] Connecting the charges to a dry cell battery and a modified wind-up alarm clock set to complete an electrical circuit at 9.45 on the morning of May 18.

33
00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:29.920
[George] Working in absolute secrecy during evening hours and weekend holidays, Keho methodically prepared the building for catastrophic demolition.

34
00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:41.000
[George] He disguised his activities under the pretense of routine plumbing maintenance and electrical repairs, answering politely whenever teachers or the custodian inquired about his presence.

35
00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:53.000
[George] Beneath the wooden floorboards, he laid hundreds of feet of copper wire, carefully splicing connections and concealing the explosive bundles behind brick piers and furnace flues where no one would suspect.

36
00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:59.000
[George] Wednesday, May 18, 1927, was a bright, idyllic spring morning.

37
00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:09.880
[George] Over 300 students and 14 teachers filled the classrooms of the Bath Consolidated School, eagerly rehearsing for eighth grade graduation and the upcoming end-of-year picnic.

38
00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:19.960
[George] At 9 45 a.m., while primary grade students in the North Wing were reciting reading lessons and practicing penmanship, the alarm clock circuit closed.

39
00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:23.160
[George] An earshattering explosion ripped through the building.

40
00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:30.600
[George] The entire north wing disintegrated in a colossal cloud of pulverized brick, mortar dust, and flying timber.

41
00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:39.560
[George] Floorboards blew skyward, and the heavy slate roof collapsed into the basement, crushing scores of young children and their teachers beneath tons of rubble.

42
00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:42.920
[George] The blast was heard ten miles away in Lansing.

43
00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:56.200
[George] Dazed survivors who staggered out into the schoolyard looked back to see half the handsome building reduced to a jagged mountain of smoking debris, while desperate screams echoed from beneath the tangled iron pipes and splintered joists.

44
00:06:56.200 --> 00:07:08.760
[George] The devastating blast tore through the north wing with seismic violence, lifting the entire second floor into the air before dropping it into a collapsed heap of bricks, broken timbers, and choking lime dust.

45
00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:14.920
[George] Classroom desks were shattered like toothpicks, and the deafening concussion blew out windows across the township.

46
00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:23.200
[George] Neighbors running to their front doors saw a colossal mushroom of black smoke rising into the bright morning sky above the schoolyard.

47
00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:36.720
[George] Within minutes, frantic parents, farmers and storekeepers rushed to the smoking ruins with shovels, crowbars and bare hands, clawing through the rubble to rescue trapped children, but Andrew Keho was not finished.

48
00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:47.520
[George] Earlier that morning, he had murdered his wife Nellie, tied her body to a wheelbarrow, set fire to all his farm buildings, and girdled his fruit trees so they would die.

49
00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:58.000
[George] He then drove his heavy Ford truck, which he had loaded with old carriage bolts, rusted chain links, rifle cartridges and dynamite into the crowded schoolyard.

50
00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:08.320
[George] As school superintendent Emory Hike ran over to confront him, Keho raised a 30-caliber rifle inside the cab and fired directly into the dynamite beside him.

51
00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:22.880
[George] The truck exploded with catastrophic force, killing Keho, Superintendent Hike, Postmaster Glenn Smith, and eight-year-old Cleo Clayton, who had survived the school collapse only to be struck by flying shrapnel in the driveway.

52
00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:30.160
[George] The truck bomb tore through rescue workers and spectators, compounding the terror in a scene of unimaginable carnage.

53
00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:39.760
[George] Heroic citizens rushed to the site with crowbars, axes and jack handles, working alongside weeping mothers to pull bleeding children from the smoking debris.

54
00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:49.280
[George] As men formed bucket brigades and carried small victims to awaiting automobiles, Keho drove his weaponized vehicle straight into the cluster of rescuers.

55
00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:58.960
[George] The subsequent explosion was a cowardly act of final defiance designed to maximize casualties among the brave townspeople who had rushed to save innocent lives.

56
00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:12.200
[George] As rescue workers and state police frantically combed through the smoldering debris of the school, emergency personnel discovered an astonishing, miraculous circumstance that prevented the death toll from being vastly worse.

57
00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:25.080
[George] In the south wing of the school, where more than 100 older high school students had been attending morning classes, Keho had wired an additional 504 pounds of unexploded dynamite and pyrotol.

58
00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:37.480
[George] State police bomb experts tracing the scorched detonator wires found that a single three-cent copper connector wire had short-circuited and burned out, failing to bridge the spark to the primary blasting cap.

59
00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:49.800
[George] Had the South Wing detonated as Keho designed, the entire structure would have collapsed simultaneously, likely killing every teacher and student in the building and doubling the catastrophic casualty count.

60
00:09:49.800 --> 00:10:00.120
[George] Bomb technicians carefully deactivated and removed the lethal charges by hand, their hands trembling as they carried crate after crate of explosives out into the schoolyard.

61
00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:08.760
[George] The discovery of 500 pounds of unexploded dynamite in the south wing brought a chilling realization to rescue workers and law enforcement.

62
00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:19.480
[George] A tiny broken connector wire, perhaps jarred loose by vibration, had prevented an even greater catastrophe that would have claimed the lives of more than 100 older students.

63
00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:27.480
[George] Experienced state police officers wept openly as they carefully removed the remaining explosive charges from the darkened crawl space.

64
00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:32.840
[George] In the days that followed, the full horror of the tragedy settled over rural Michigan.

65
00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:43.000
[George] Forty-five people died in total, including 38 elementary school children and six adults, while another 58 were severely maimed or permanently crippled.

66
00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:53.760
[George] The town held 17 separate funerals in a single day, with small white coffins laid out in living rooms, churches, and country cemeteries across Clinton County.

67
00:10:53.760 --> 00:11:02.640
[George] When investigators searched Keho Chard Farm ruins, they found a wooden sign stenciled with his chilling final message wired to the boundary fence.

68
00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:05.120
[George] Criminals are made, not born.

69
00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:08.000
[George] The nation rallied around the shattered township.

70
00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:22.880
[George] Over $50,000 in donations poured in from across America, and Senator James Cousins personally donated $75,000 to construct a new, modern school on the site, named the James Cousins Agricultural School.

71
00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:32.320
[George] In 1975, a peaceful memorial park was established on the grounds of the former school, featuring a bronze cupola from the original building.

72
00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:46.560
[George] Today, visitors walk the quiet green park in Bath, honoring the innocent children and dedicated teachers whose lives were stolen, remembering the resilience of a small Midwestern town that refused to let hatred have the final word.

73
00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:54.160
[George] In the aftermath of the disaster, Bath Township became a symbol of nationwide mourning and profound community strength.

74
00:11:54.160 --> 00:12:02.560
[George] Thousands of sympathy letters, toys and donations poured into the small post office from every state in the Union and several foreign countries.

75
00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:23.160
[George] The resilient people of Bath refused to be broken by one man's evil, uniting to rebuild their community on foundations of love, compassion, and shared reminder of the fragility of innocence and the courage of community resilience.

76
00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:24.520
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

77
00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:29.520
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we journey to the utopian frontier of New Harmony, Indiana.

